WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:01.768
-Welcome back.

00:00:01.768 --> 00:00:03.369
Our next guest is
an Emmy-nominated actress

00:00:03.369 --> 00:00:06.372
you know from films such as
"Once Upon a Time in Hollywood"

00:00:06.372 --> 00:00:08.808
and shows like "Fosse/Verdon"
and "The Leftovers."

00:00:08.808 --> 00:00:12.111
She was nominated for both
a Golden Globe and a SAG Award

00:00:12.111 --> 00:00:13.779
for her performance in "Maid,"

00:00:13.779 --> 00:00:15.281
which is streaming now
on Netflix.

00:00:15.281 --> 00:00:16.449
Let's take a look.

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:19.351
-The point is, honey, ah --

00:00:19.351 --> 00:00:22.188
you know, I like being a gypsy.

00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:24.356
I like being mobile, okay?

00:00:24.356 --> 00:00:26.192
You know me. I'm scrappy.

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:27.693
-I don't know what to do.
I don't know what to do.

00:00:27.693 --> 00:00:29.028
I can't afford to put you
in a motel.

00:00:29.028 --> 00:00:30.196
I can't bring you
to the shelter.

00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:31.697
I don't know what to do, Mom.

00:00:31.697 --> 00:00:33.933
-I am not your problem,
Alexandra.

00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:35.101
-Yes, you are.

00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:36.602
-No, I am not.

00:00:36.602 --> 00:00:38.171
I'm your mother.
-No, you're not.

00:00:38.171 --> 00:00:40.340
-Yes, I am!

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:41.841
I am.

00:00:41.841 --> 00:00:44.344
-Please welcome to the show
Margaret Qualley.

00:00:44.344 --> 00:00:46.345
[ Applause ]

00:00:46.345 --> 00:00:52.785 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:52.785 --> 00:00:59.258 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:59.258 --> 00:01:00.592
Welcome to the show!

00:01:00.592 --> 00:01:02.629
-Thank you so much.
-I'm so happy to have you here.

00:01:02.629 --> 00:01:04.430
Now, that was your real mom.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:07.934
-So, yeah, she says, "You're not
my mom," but she really is.

00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:09.268
-In real life.
-Yeah.

00:01:09.268 --> 00:01:11.938
-Andie MacDowell is your mom.
And this is -- you --

00:01:11.938 --> 00:01:13.506
Obviously, a very
accomplished actress,

00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:14.841
but you helped
get her this part.

00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:15.975
That's a big deal.
-I know.

00:01:15.975 --> 00:01:17.810
How crazy lucky am I, right?
[ Laughs ] Yeah.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:20.413
-So did you suggest
to the producers,

00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:21.881
"Hey, you know who'd be good
at my mom?

00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:23.048
My mom."
-My mom. Yeah.

00:01:23.048 --> 00:01:25.384
There's no real argument
for that, you know?

00:01:25.384 --> 00:01:28.388
Like, what are you going to say?
"I don't believe that"?

00:01:28.388 --> 00:01:31.056
-Yeah, she's not
a believable mom at all.

00:01:31.056 --> 00:01:33.559
Do you remember
the first time as a kid

00:01:33.559 --> 00:01:35.728
seeing your mom on screen?

00:01:35.728 --> 00:01:37.096
Was it weird?

00:01:37.096 --> 00:01:39.432
-Um, it certainly was. Yeah.

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:41.967
I, um...yeah.

00:01:41.967 --> 00:01:45.171
I think the first time I really
remember it is actually,

00:01:45.171 --> 00:01:48.674
it's a pretty vivid memory,
because my mom was going on

00:01:48.674 --> 00:01:50.510
"Sesame Street,"
kind of on my behalf,

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:51.844
because it seemed like
a sweet thing to do.

00:01:51.844 --> 00:01:54.513
-Sure.
-Yeah, so sweet.

00:01:54.513 --> 00:01:56.950
But the way that I interpreted
it as a four-year-old --

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.151
well, she was playing --
she's from the South.

00:01:59.151 --> 00:02:01.154
I am, too.

00:02:01.154 --> 00:02:02.955
[ Laughing ]
'Cause she's my mom.

00:02:02.955 --> 00:02:05.925
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:05.925 --> 00:02:08.528
-Anyway, so she was playing
a Southern belle

00:02:08.528 --> 00:02:10.262
stuck in a bell tower
on "Sesame Street,"

00:02:10.262 --> 00:02:13.099
but she was away from home
while she was doing it,

00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:15.434
and my four-year-old mind
was just like,

00:02:15.434 --> 00:02:17.436
"My mom's stuck
in a bell tower."

00:02:17.436 --> 00:02:20.105
You know?
Like, "This is awful."

00:02:20.105 --> 00:02:22.609
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:02:22.609 --> 00:02:24.410
Those muppets,
that can backfire on you.

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:25.745
Yeah.
-It was terrifying.

00:02:25.745 --> 00:02:27.613
-It could be terrifying, yeah.
Very much so.

00:02:27.613 --> 00:02:30.616
You are nominated
for a SAG Award.

00:02:30.616 --> 00:02:31.951
Congratulations very much on
that.

00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:33.686
-Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:35.188
Thanks.

00:02:35.188 --> 00:02:38.858
-And, you know, obviously a very
exciting time for your career.

00:02:38.858 --> 00:02:42.195
I'm also aware that you had
a very tragic loss recently.

00:02:42.195 --> 00:02:44.030
-I did, yeah.
-Someone who was incredibly

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.032
supportive of your career
and integral to it.

00:02:46.032 --> 00:02:47.733
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:02:47.733 --> 00:02:49.068
You know, to be honest,

00:02:49.068 --> 00:02:51.037
I probably wouldn't be here
if it w--

00:02:51.037 --> 00:02:53.206
I lost my manager
a couple of weeks ago.

00:02:53.206 --> 00:02:55.375
He took his own life,
and it's just awful.

00:02:55.375 --> 00:02:57.042
And I probably wouldn't be doing
something like this,

00:02:57.042 --> 00:02:59.846
but I think that like, because
he's my manager, you know,

00:02:59.846 --> 00:03:01.948
he'd be really pissed off
if I weren't doing this.

00:03:01.948 --> 00:03:03.282
[ Laughing ]

00:03:03.282 --> 00:03:04.617
So, but...

00:03:04.617 --> 00:03:10.123
But yeah, his -- his family
actually started

00:03:10.123 --> 00:03:13.126
a foundation on his behalf
with NAMI,

00:03:13.126 --> 00:03:16.128
the National Alliance
for Mental Illness,

00:03:16.128 --> 00:03:19.799
so if anybody wants to donate
to that, that's amazing.

00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:23.603
But another thing to do is just
to tell the people that you love

00:03:23.603 --> 00:03:30.343
that you love them, and just if
you are suffering, to seek help,

00:03:30.343 --> 00:03:33.012
because there's people out there
that really want to help you.

00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:34.847
And there you go.

00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:36.515
-Well, thanks for being here.
-I've been great.

00:03:36.515 --> 00:03:39.352
[ Laughs ]

00:03:39.352 --> 00:03:45.191
-I can only imagine how proud
he was of you and your career.

00:03:45.191 --> 00:03:47.259
And I also have heard that
he was also very supportive

00:03:47.259 --> 00:03:48.761
on your behalf on social media.

00:03:48.761 --> 00:03:52.431
-Oh, yeah.
Oh, yes, he was.

00:03:52.431 --> 00:03:54.266
I mean, everybody loves Chris.

00:03:54.266 --> 00:03:56.102
He's one of those, like,
real-life angels.

00:03:56.102 --> 00:03:59.272
Anybody that ever was
in his orbit just made --

00:03:59.272 --> 00:04:02.107
he made them feel
like they were family.

00:04:02.107 --> 00:04:05.744
But with me, he's had my back
for the past 10 years,

00:04:05.744 --> 00:04:09.882
but his Instagram presence
was particularly special

00:04:09.882 --> 00:04:12.718
because he would --
you know, sometimes people --

00:04:12.718 --> 00:04:14.520
people on the internet
aren't that nice sometimes.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:18.290
-No, they're not, no.
-Shocking.

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:21.127
But when people would say
mean things

00:04:21.127 --> 00:04:24.163
on a photo of me on Instagram,

00:04:24.163 --> 00:04:27.099
he would personally go through
each comment

00:04:27.099 --> 00:04:28.935
and then attack them

00:04:28.935 --> 00:04:33.840
and like, go so far as to
provide his location

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.878
like, "I'm on the corner of
La Brea and some other street

00:04:38.878 --> 00:04:42.381
in Hollywood, and I'm driving
this car and this is my outfit."

00:04:42.381 --> 00:04:44.617
Like, "Come find me
and I'll tell you

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:46.652
what kind of a person
Margaret really is."

00:04:46.652 --> 00:04:48.154
[ Laughter ]

00:04:48.154 --> 00:04:49.322
So, yeah.

00:04:49.322 --> 00:04:51.424
-Well, that is quite a presence.
-Really sweet.

00:04:51.424 --> 00:04:55.094
-This show is a really
special show.

00:04:55.094 --> 00:04:57.263
You have a -- there's an actor

00:04:57.263 --> 00:04:58.731
who's not your actual
four-year-old.

00:04:58.731 --> 00:05:00.866
You've got your real mom, but
not your real four-year-old.

00:05:00.866 --> 00:05:02.201
-Yes, I haven't had
one of those.

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:04.503
-But a four-year-old actor
who's wonderful in it.

00:05:04.503 --> 00:05:06.305
-Yes.
-But I would imagine,

00:05:06.305 --> 00:05:08.307
especially for a show like this
that's so dramatic,

00:05:08.307 --> 00:05:10.977
how do you keep
a four-year-old's attention

00:05:10.977 --> 00:05:13.979
over the course of a day of
shooting, which are long days?

00:05:13.979 --> 00:05:16.281
-It was hard.

00:05:16.281 --> 00:05:19.619
I love her so much, Rylea.
So, so great.

00:05:19.619 --> 00:05:21.620
But, and you know, to be honest,

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:24.623
the thing is that
I would just like --

00:05:24.623 --> 00:05:28.294
I would really use the crew
in kind of like a mean way.

00:05:28.294 --> 00:05:30.295
I mean, I love the crew so much,

00:05:30.295 --> 00:05:34.233
this group of incredible people
up in Vancouver Island.

00:05:34.233 --> 00:05:37.903
But you know, late at night
after 12 hours of work

00:05:37.903 --> 00:05:39.305
and you want to go home

00:05:39.305 --> 00:05:42.408
and Rylea just has one line
to say that's like --

00:05:42.408 --> 00:05:45.411
Okay, one time,
it was at the end of,

00:05:45.411 --> 00:05:48.247
I don't remember the episode,
seven or something like that,

00:05:48.247 --> 00:05:50.916
and the line is like,
"Best day ever, Mommy."

00:05:50.916 --> 00:05:52.852
And she wouldn't say it.
I guess she didn't have

00:05:52.852 --> 00:05:55.621
a good day, and she was like,
"That's not authentic."

00:05:55.621 --> 00:05:56.989
[ Laughter ]

00:05:56.989 --> 00:05:58.491
But we were all just,
like, really trying

00:05:58.491 --> 00:06:00.526
to get her to say
"best day ever."

00:06:00.526 --> 00:06:03.729
And so we would enlist
various crew members

00:06:03.729 --> 00:06:05.731
to do just ridiculous things.

00:06:05.731 --> 00:06:09.368
I remember calling Jess over,
who's a sound guy,

00:06:09.368 --> 00:06:11.204
to dance for her,

00:06:11.204 --> 00:06:14.339
and just anybody to do
absolutely anything.

00:06:14.339 --> 00:06:16.008
Special man C-- Um...

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:19.645
I just called him "special man
Cam", but that was his nickname,

00:06:19.645 --> 00:06:21.146
was the second AD's nickname.

00:06:21.146 --> 00:06:23.515
I mean, really, it was --
kids are [bleep]

00:06:23.515 --> 00:06:25.184
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:25.184 --> 00:06:26.853
Yeah, no.
-Like, don't work with them.

00:06:26.853 --> 00:06:28.354
-Why I asked that question,

00:06:28.354 --> 00:06:30.857
'cause I have a kid
who's almost 4.

00:06:30.857 --> 00:06:34.527
-Yeah.
-And the idea that I could get

00:06:34.527 --> 00:06:36.028
him to say something...
-Right.

00:06:36.028 --> 00:06:37.530
I mean, the amount of power

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:39.531
that they have on a set
is outrageous.

00:06:39.531 --> 00:06:42.201
-Yeah. Or, by the way,
everywhere else.

00:06:42.201 --> 00:06:43.402
-Right. That's true.

00:06:43.402 --> 00:06:44.770
That's true.
-[ Laughing ] Yeah.

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:46.305
-In the beginning,
she was such a sweetheart,

00:06:46.305 --> 00:06:48.574
and then by the end of --
it was nine months up there,

00:06:48.574 --> 00:06:50.242
she realized that, like,

00:06:50.242 --> 00:06:52.245
everyone had to do
what she wanted.

00:06:52.245 --> 00:06:53.579
-Yeah.
-You know? I mean, I love her.

00:06:53.579 --> 00:06:54.913
Bless her.
Bless her, bless her, bless her.

00:06:54.913 --> 00:06:57.749
-No, no, that's coming across,
how much you love her.

00:06:57.749 --> 00:06:59.585
Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:59.585 --> 00:07:02.722
I mean, she's a power-mad
tyrant, but she's --

00:07:02.722 --> 00:07:04.824
oh, my God, she's my heart.

00:07:04.824 --> 00:07:06.659
[ Laughter ]

00:07:06.659 --> 00:07:08.493
We have something in common.

00:07:08.493 --> 00:07:09.662
We adore Adam Sandler.

00:07:09.662 --> 00:07:11.097
-Oh, yeah, I do.

00:07:11.097 --> 00:07:13.765
-And I thought,
as Adam Sandler fans went,

00:07:13.765 --> 00:07:16.101
I was right at the top
of the list, but you...

00:07:16.101 --> 00:07:17.937
-And then you met me.
-I met you.

00:07:17.937 --> 00:07:19.938
Well, I didn't meet you as much
as I heard that you're --

00:07:19.938 --> 00:07:22.275
basically, like, this is what
you get for Christmas presents.

00:07:22.275 --> 00:07:24.243
-Yes. Yeah.

00:07:24.243 --> 00:07:26.746
-So this is, by the way,
not a signed photograph

00:07:26.746 --> 00:07:28.747
from Adam Sandler.
-No, it's not.

00:07:28.747 --> 00:07:30.215
-You got his co-star.

00:07:30.215 --> 00:07:31.550
-Mr. Larson.
-Yeah.

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:33.052
[ Laughter ]

00:07:33.052 --> 00:07:34.487
So, people in your life knew

00:07:34.487 --> 00:07:36.555
that this would be
an appropriate Christmas gift.

00:07:36.555 --> 00:07:38.791
-Yeah, my boyfriend's sister,
Rachel, gave that to me,

00:07:38.791 --> 00:07:40.959
and I'm getting it framed
right now.

00:07:40.959 --> 00:07:43.295
It's very exciting.

00:07:43.295 --> 00:07:44.663
And yeah, it was.

00:07:44.663 --> 00:07:46.665
It was a really great gift,
but it didn't stop there.

00:07:46.665 --> 00:07:50.169
-Okay, yeah, no, this was...
this is a T-shirt.

00:07:50.169 --> 00:07:52.471
-My boyfriend gave me that.
-Okay, that's beautiful.

00:07:52.471 --> 00:07:53.505
-Yeah, yeah.

00:07:53.505 --> 00:07:54.840
-So, you did really well
for yourself.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:56.508
-I did really well.
-That's quite a haul.

00:07:56.508 --> 00:07:59.277
-It was a really exciting
Christmas, 'cause I got those,

00:07:59.277 --> 00:08:02.147
and then I also gave some

00:08:02.147 --> 00:08:04.283
"Happy Gilmore" paraphernalia,
as well.

00:08:04.283 --> 00:08:06.118
I gave some Subway shirts out,
you know?

00:08:06.118 --> 00:08:07.553
-Yeah.

00:08:07.553 --> 00:08:10.556
-If you're a hard fan, you'll
know what I'm talking about.

00:08:10.556 --> 00:08:12.558
I think I might name
my first child --

00:08:12.558 --> 00:08:13.893
hopefully, they're not
an [bleep] --

00:08:13.893 --> 00:08:14.893
-- "Sonny."

00:08:14.893 --> 00:08:16.561
-Sonny?
-I don't know. Yeah.

00:08:16.561 --> 00:08:19.231
-It would at least make it funny
if they were an [bleep]

00:08:19.231 --> 00:08:21.567
-Right, but I was bringing it
back to the Rylea thing.

00:08:21.567 --> 00:08:24.236
-Yes, of course. Of course.
No, no, I knew.

00:08:24.236 --> 00:08:25.738
I know you're not just like,
out of nowhere,

00:08:25.738 --> 00:08:28.073
just saying, "Kids are [bleep]"
-Yeah, yeah.

00:08:28.073 --> 00:08:30.242
-You've established.
You've laid down proof.

00:08:30.242 --> 00:08:32.911
Hey, you put this on Instagram,

00:08:32.911 --> 00:08:37.416
and this is not your pet,
this photo, right?

00:08:37.416 --> 00:08:39.718
-No, that's not my pet.
-Okay, but it looks like

00:08:39.718 --> 00:08:41.387
you allowed it to get
a lot closer

00:08:41.387 --> 00:08:43.188
than I would allow
this animal to get.

00:08:43.188 --> 00:08:45.858
So, where is this and why did
you let this happen?

00:08:45.858 --> 00:08:47.860
-I mean, I was at the park.

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:49.361
-Okay, you're at a park
in New York City.

00:08:49.361 --> 00:08:50.862
-I was at the park
in New York City.

00:08:50.862 --> 00:08:52.698
-Yeah, no, but the question is,
why didn't you move your foot?

00:08:52.698 --> 00:08:55.100
-I mean, to be honest,
I thought about...

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:57.770
Oh, why didn't I move my foot?
Well, I want to be close to it.

00:08:57.770 --> 00:08:59.271
Of course!
-You did want to be close to it.

00:08:59.271 --> 00:09:00.439
-Of course.

00:09:00.439 --> 00:09:02.108
-Okay, so I think that's
the story here, Margaret.

00:09:02.108 --> 00:09:04.243
-I know, but I feel bad, because
the end of this story --

00:09:04.243 --> 00:09:05.944
well, I've never really
told anyone this,

00:09:05.944 --> 00:09:08.947
but the end of this story
is that the poor little guy,

00:09:08.947 --> 00:09:10.615
like, wanted to be my friend,
but I didn't really --

00:09:10.615 --> 00:09:11.950
like, I could have picked it up
or something,

00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:13.785
but I thought maybe it could
have rabies.

00:09:13.785 --> 00:09:15.955
-It 100% has rabies.

00:09:15.955 --> 00:09:16.956
[ Laughter ]

00:09:16.956 --> 00:09:18.623
What are you talking about?

00:09:18.623 --> 00:09:20.626
You know, I mean, I think
that's the first thing,

00:09:20.626 --> 00:09:22.495
like, when you land
at LaGuardia,

00:09:22.495 --> 00:09:24.830
they're like, "Don't take
the squirrels home."

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:26.832
-Yeah, no, but it kind of
followed me.

00:09:26.832 --> 00:09:28.501
Like, I left and it was
following me,

00:09:28.501 --> 00:09:30.603
and I just felt bad because
I was like, maybe the squirrel

00:09:30.603 --> 00:09:32.137
needs something from me,
maybe it's ask--

00:09:32.137 --> 00:09:34.473
But I didn't pick it up
because of the rabies thing.

00:09:34.473 --> 00:09:38.477
-On behalf of all of us,
thank you for making

00:09:38.477 --> 00:09:42.147
the right decision
when the moment of truth came.

00:09:42.147 --> 00:09:45.450
But we do have a very special
surprise for you.

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:46.452
The squirrel's here.

00:09:46.452 --> 00:09:47.786
No, I'm kidding.
-Oh.

00:09:47.786 --> 00:09:50.121
God, I really thought
Adam Sandler was gonna walk out.

00:09:50.121 --> 00:09:51.290
Terrible day.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:53.459
-Sorry about that.
Yeah, we really blew it.

00:09:53.459 --> 00:09:55.461
But can you please,
just before we go,

00:09:55.461 --> 00:09:56.928
